---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../lib/site";
---
<Base title="How this website works" description="Operations manual for brbcbaytown.org" noindex={true}>
  <div class="phead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Internal · unlisted</p>
      <h1>How this website works.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything needed to run brbcbaytown.org, fix it, or hand it to somebody else.
        Written 7 September 2026. If you are new to this, read Part 1 and Part 2 and stop.
        Part 3 is there for the day it is needed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sec"><div class="wrap doc">

    <div class="startbox">
      <h2 class="startbox-h">Start here</h2>
      <p><strong>There is one weekly job: post the Beacon.</strong> That is Part 1, and it takes a
      minute. Everything else on this site either never changes or updates itself — sermons arrive
      from YouTube every Monday, events arrive from Planning Center every night.</p>
      <p><strong>To change words, times, or links,</strong> go to
      <a href="https://brbcbaytown.org/admin"><strong>brbcbaytown.org/admin</strong></a>, sign in as
      <strong><code>connect@brbcbaytown.org</code></strong> with the normal ministry password, and edit
      like a form. Click Save. That <em>is</em> publishing — there is no publish button. Give it a few
      minutes. That is Part 2.</p>
      <p><strong>If something is broken</strong> — the site is down, a save will not appear, sermons
      stopped, an event is missing — go straight to
      <a href="#broken"><strong>the troubleshooting table in Part 3</strong></a>.</p>
      <p class="startbox-owner"><strong>Who owns this site today:</strong> Sarah Rose. Passwords and
      keys are not on this page and never will be — they are on Matt Rose's Mac in
      <code>Baker Road Baptist/STORED ACCOUNTS/</code>. Every service listed in Part 3 also has a
      normal "forgot password" flow.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="toolbar">
      <button type="button" id="expandAll" class="expandbtn">Open every section</button>
      <span class="toolbar-hint">Use this before printing, or before searching the page with Ctrl+F.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <p><strong>This page is unlisted, not private.</strong> It is not linked from the menu,
      the footer, or the sitemap, and search engines are told not to index it. But anyone
      who has the address can read it. So it names every account and says where the keys
      are kept — <strong>it never contains a password, token, or key itself.</strong> Do not add one.</p>
    </div>
  </div></section>

  <!-- ══════════════════ PART 1 ══════════════════ -->

  <section class="sec" id="weekly"><div class="wrap doc">
    <p class="partlabel">Part 1</p>
    <h2 class="parth">The weekly job</h2>
    <p class="partlede">Post that Sunday's Beacon. This is the only thing on the whole site with a
    weekly rhythm, and it is the only thing that will go stale if nobody does it.</p>

    <h3>Every week — put up the Beacon</h3>
    <p>The office already makes The Beacon every week as a set of full-page pictures. The site does
    not try to rebuild that in a web page — it is a place to put the pictures, and nothing more.
    One issue is one entry, named after its Sunday.</p>
    <p><strong>Before you start:</strong> have this week's Beacon pages saved somewhere you can find
    them, one file per page, <strong>saved as JPEG</strong>. There is nothing else to prepare.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to <strong>brbcbaytown.org/admin</strong> and sign in as
        <strong><code>connect@brbcbaytown.org</code></strong> with the normal ministry password.</li>
      <li>In the list down the left, click <strong>5. The Beacon (weekly newsletter)</strong>.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Add File</strong>, top right.</li>
      <li>The date box already shows <strong>the coming Sunday</strong>. Nearly every week that is
        right and you can leave it. Change it only if you are doing a different week.</li>
      <li>Find <strong>The pictures</strong> and click <strong>Add item</strong>. Click the
        <em>Picture</em> box and upload the <strong>front page</strong>.</li>
      <li>In <strong>What is on this page?</strong> type a short description, e.g.
        <em>"Front page: today's sermon, Sunday classes and the week's schedule."</em> Somebody who
        cannot see hears only those words.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Add item</strong> again and do the same for the back page. Repeat for a
        third page if there is one.</li>
      <li><em>Optional, about a minute:</em> fill in <strong>One line about this week</strong> and
        two or three <strong>things worth typing out</strong>. See the fold below for why they help.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Save</strong>, top right.</li>
      <li>Wait a few minutes, then open <strong>brbcbaytown.org/upcoming/</strong> and hard-refresh
        with <code>Cmd+Shift+R</code>. Your issue should be there.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>That is the whole job, and last week's comes down by itself.</strong> There is no
      publish button, no archive to tidy, and nothing to delete. The address is always
      <code>brbcbaytown.org/upcoming/</code>, so that is the link to share on Facebook or in a
      text, and it is always the current week. The old address
      <code>brbcbaytown.org/newsletter/</code> still works and lands in the same place, because
      that is the word people go looking for.</p>
    </div>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>The Beacon looks wrong — the four things that actually happen</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <div class="tblwrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>What it means</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>The page shows the words but no pictures</td><td>An upload did not finish. The site
              shows the text rather than a broken-image box.</td><td>Open the issue in Tina, upload the
              picture again, Save.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Still last week's issue after ten minutes</td><td>Either the save did not go
              through, or your browser is showing you an old copy.</td><td>Hard-refresh
              (<code>Cmd+Shift+R</code>). If it is still wrong, check the issue is really in the list in
              Tina, then see <a href="#broken">the troubleshooting table</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>You picked the wrong Sunday</td><td>Nothing is broken.</td><td>Open the issue, fix
              the date, Save. The website goes by the date you set, not by the file's name.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>You want the Beacon off the page entirely</td><td>Nothing replaces it this
              week.</td><td>Open the issue in Tina, use the three-dot menu on the right, Delete. The
              page then says the next one will appear shortly.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>The page shows its text and quietly skips a picture whose file is missing, rather than
        showing a broken-image icon. So if an issue looks bare, the upload did not finish — open the
        issue in Tina, re-upload, and save.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>Why JPEG, and the two optional boxes</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p><strong>Save the pictures as JPEG, not PNG.</strong> A full page exported as a PNG can be ten
        megabytes and takes a long time to open on a phone on a car-park signal. The same page as a
        JPEG is under one megabyte and looks identical.</p>
        <p>A picture of a page cannot be read aloud by a phone, cannot be searched, and on a 375-pixel
        screen renders about 330 pixels wide — unreadable without pinching. Every page picture therefore
        links to itself at full size, which is the pinch-and-zoom fallback. The two text boxes are the
        better fix:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One line about this week</strong> — shown under the date, and the text that appears
            when somebody shares the link in a text message or on Facebook.</li>
          <li><strong>A few things worth typing out</strong> — three or four items nobody should miss.
            This is the part a screen reader reads and a phone can enlarge. Leave it empty on a busy
            week; the pictures still work on their own.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>Why only one issue is ever up, and what Google is allowed to see</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p>The page shows <strong>one</strong> issue: the newest. Posting this Sunday's is what takes
        last Sunday's down. There is no archive to prune and no old link to go and delete, because a
        bulletin is the most perishable thing the church produces — a rota, a set of times and a list
        of names, true for seven days. A stack of old ones is a stack of things that are no longer so.</p>
        <p>Nothing is actually deleted. An unpublished issue keeps its file in the repository and its
        place in the history, so it can be read or brought back at any time. It simply stops being the
        page. If you want the files tidied as well, delete the old entry in Tina; the website will not
        notice either way.</p>
        <p>Because only one issue is ever published, the address is always the same:
        <code>brbcbaytown.org/upcoming/</code>. That is the link to put on Facebook or in a text, and
        it is always this week's. The old <code>/newsletter/</code> address and the dated per-issue
        addresses that existed briefly on 10 September 2026 all redirect here.</p>
        <p><strong>Google indexes this page.</strong> It did not until 10 September 2026, when the
        church decided the events on it are worth being found — a church's calendar is exactly what
        somebody searches for. The page carries schema.org Event data so those events can show as
        rich results.</p>
        <p><strong>One thing is still held back: the pictures.</strong> The page sends
        <code>noimageindex</code>, so the Beacon page-scans never enter Google Images. Those scans
        carry the children's ministry rota, volunteer names and the week's giving figures, and there
        is no reason for a 2026 rota to sit in image search in 2031. The page's own words carry none
        of that — only the date, the summary, the highlights and the event names — so indexing the
        text costs nothing.</p>
        <p>To change it either way: <code>robots="noimageindex"</code> in
        <code>src/pages/upcoming.astro</code> is the switch, and the sitemap filter in
        <code>astro.config.mjs</code> now excludes only <code>/sop</code>. Putting the page back out
        of Google means <code>noindex={true}</code> there and <code>/upcoming</code> back in the
        filter. Note that un-indexing is slow: Google drops a page in its own time.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>Doing the Beacon from a terminal instead</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p>For whoever is already in the code rather than in Tina. It converts to JPEG, caps the width
        at 2000px, names the files after the Sunday, and leaves any summary and highlights already on
        that issue alone:</p>
        <pre><code>node scripts/add-beacon.mjs 2026-09-06 ~/Desktop/page1.png ~/Desktop/page2.png</code></pre>
      </div>
    </details>

    <h3>The rest of the year, in full</h3>
    <p>This is the entire recurring workload of the website. Put these on a real calendar.</p>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Every week</strong></td><td>Put up that Sunday's Beacon. About a minute; the steps are above.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Every September</strong></td><td>Add this year's Pumpkin Patch to Planning Center and tag it <code>Website</code>. That is the whole yearly event workload.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Every January</strong></td><td>Read the whole site once, out loud. Check every time, name, and role is still true — pastor's bio, staff, deacons, class names.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Before 5 February 2028</strong></td><td>Renew the domain at Network Solutions. Set the reminder now. If it lapses, the whole site vanishes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Whenever staff change</strong></td><td><strong>Change the password on <code>connect@brbcbaytown.org</code></strong>, since that is the login everybody shares. Then check the Tina editor seats (there are two), the GitHub and Cloudflare admins, and rotate the Planning Center token if the person who created it leaves.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </div></section>

  <!-- ══════════════════ PART 2 ══════════════════ -->

  <section class="sec" id="editing"><div class="wrap doc">
    <p class="partlabel">Part 2</p>
    <h2 class="parth">Editing the site</h2>
    <p class="partlede">Written for whoever ends up doing this. You do not need to know anything about
    computers beyond filling in a form. Everything on the website is edited in one place —
    except events, which are added in Planning Center, and sermons, which nobody adds at all.</p>

    <h3>Getting in</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to <strong>brbcbaytown.org/admin</strong>.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Log in with TinaCloud</strong>. Sign in as
        <strong><code>connect@brbcbaytown.org</code></strong> with <strong>the normal ministry
        password</strong> — the one the office already uses for the church's other accounts. That is
        the main login for the editor and the one to hand on. If it does not work, the password is in
        <code>STORED ACCOUNTS</code> (see Part 3); it is deliberately not written on this page.</li>
      <li>If that login has been removed, or you need a second person editing at the same time,
        whoever runs <code>bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</code> adds an address at app.tina.io → project
        → Collaborators. <strong>There are only two seats</strong> on the free plan; a third costs
        money. Share the <code>connect@</code> login rather than buy one.</li>
      <li>You land on "Welcome to Tina!" with a menu on the left. That menu is the entire website.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>The five things in the left menu</h3>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Menu item</th><th>What is in it</th><th>Edit it?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>1. Church Info</strong></td><td>Address, phone, office hours, the Sunday and Wednesday schedules, links to Facebook, YouTube, giving, the app, and the two forms. <strong>Change a time here and it changes everywhere on the site at once.</strong> This is the one you will use most.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2. Pages</strong></td><td>One entry per page: home, visit, about, ministries, watch, upcoming, give, contact, privacy-policy. Every box is plain text.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>3. Sermons</strong></td><td>One entry per sermon, written automatically from YouTube every Monday.</td><td>Only to fix a title or series. Do not add or delete.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>4. Events</strong></td><td>Written automatically from Planning Center every night.</td><td>Only to tick "show on homepage" or add a picture. Do not add or delete — see below.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>5. The Beacon</strong></td><td>The weekly newsletter, one entry per Sunday.</td><td>Yes — you add one a week. See <a href="#weekly">Part 1</a>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h3>To change a service time</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Left menu → <strong>1. Church Info</strong> → click the single entry.</li>
      <li>Scroll to <strong>Sunday schedule</strong> or <strong>Wednesday schedule</strong>. Each row has
        <em>What it is</em>, <em>Small note underneath</em>, and <em>Time</em>.</li>
      <li>Change the text. Click <strong>Save</strong> (top right). Wait a few minutes. Hard-refresh the site.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>To change the words on a page</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Left menu → <strong>2. Pages</strong> → click the page.</li>
      <li>Each page has a <em>Headline</em>, an <em>Opening paragraph</em>, and a list of <em>Sections</em>.
        Click a section to open it. Inside a section are a heading, a paragraph, and sometimes a list of
        <em>items</em> — the cards you see on the site. Each card has a title, a small label, a description,
        a "when it meets" line, and up to two buttons.</li>
      <li>Type in the box like an email. Click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>To add an event — this is Planning Center, not Tina</h3>
    <p>Events on the website come <strong>from Planning Center</strong>. You never type an event
    into Tina.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>In Planning Center Calendar, create the event as normal (name, dates, description).</li>
      <li>Under Settings → Church Center, set <strong>Visibility: Published</strong> and add a picture if
        you want one on the site.</li>
      <li>At the top of the event, <strong>Add tag → Website</strong>. <strong>This tag is the on-switch.</strong>
        No tag, no website.</li>
      <li>It appears on the site the next morning (the sync runs nightly, about 5–6 am).</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Sermons need nothing at all</h3>
    <p>Every Monday around midday a robot reads the church's YouTube channel and writes any new video
    onto the Watch page. Nothing to do week to week. If a title comes across wrong, fix it in Tina →
    <strong>3. Sermons</strong> → fix the title or series → Save. Your fix survives every future
    Monday sync; the robot notices a human changed it and leaves it alone.
    If sermons stop appearing entirely, see <a href="#broken">the troubleshooting table</a>.</p>

    <h3>What not to do in Tina</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Do not add or delete Sermons or Events by hand. The robots own those lists and will fight you.</li>
      <li>Do not change anything under <strong>Church Info</strong> that ends in <code>Url</code> unless the
        service itself gave you a new address.</li>
      <li>Do not go looking for a page builder or a layout tool. There is not one. Every field is a plain
        text box, deliberately, so that a form-filler can run this site.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>If a save does not appear</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Give it a full ten minutes. Then hard-refresh (<code>Cmd+Shift+R</code> on a Mac, or open a
        private window).</li>
      <li>Cloudflare → website → Deployments. Is the top build green? If red, see
        <a href="#broken">the troubleshooting table</a>.</li>
      <li>GitHub → the repo → is your change the newest commit? If not, the save did not reach GitHub —
        log out of Tina and back in.</li>
    </ol>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>Adding pictures, and the rule about photos of children</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p>Only a few places take a picture: a card on a page, or an event. Open it, click the
        <em>Picture</em> field, upload. Landscape photos work best. <strong>Do not put a photo of a
        child on the site without a parent's permission</strong>, and do not use a photo somebody else
        took without asking them. As of launch, only the Pumpkin Patch has a real photo; the other
        ministries are waiting for someone to take one on a Sunday.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>The two buttons on every ministry card, and the @ shortcuts</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p>They are not typed in by hand. Each button's link is written as <code>@connectionCardUrl</code>
        or <code>@serveFormUrl</code>, which means "look this up in Church Info". So if Planning Center ever
        gives you a new form address, change it <strong>once</strong> in Church Info and all fourteen buttons
        follow. Other lookups that work the same way: <code>@mailto</code> (the church email),
        <code>@tel</code> (the phone), <code>@givingUrl</code>, <code>@facebookUrl</code>, <code>@youtubeUrl</code>,
        <code>@appUrl</code>, <code>@mapUrl</code>.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>Why events need a tag, and what the sermon robot actually does</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p><strong>Why the tag.</strong> When the site was built, Planning Center's
        "visible in Church Center" box was ticked on 123 of 136 events — including two weddings, a
        memorial service, and the Personnel Committee. So the website ignores that box and publishes only
        what is deliberately tagged. It also refuses, by name, the outside groups that rent the building
        and anything that reads as a wedding, funeral, or committee. Weekly things — Sunday School, Bible
        study — are held back as "the schedule", which is already on every page.</p>
        <p><strong>The sermon robot.</strong> Every Monday around midday, a job on GitHub reads the
        church's YouTube channel — its free public RSS feed, no key or account needed — and writes any new
        video onto the Watch page. It reads the date out of the title in whatever format it was typed,
        de-shouts ALL-CAPS titles, and sorts videos into series using the list in
        <code>content/sermon-series.json</code>. Only <strong>public</strong> videos are used — unlisted and
        private ones are skipped. If a whole series is being missed, add its name to that file in GitHub
        (it is a list of known series names and their misspellings).</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div></section>

  <!-- ══════════════════ PART 3 ══════════════════ -->

  <section class="sec deepsec" id="ecosystem"><div class="wrap doc">
    <p class="partlabel">Part 3</p>
    <h2 class="parth">Everything underneath</h2>
    <p class="partlede">Nobody needs this to run the website week to week. It is here for the day
    something breaks, an account has to be recovered, or the whole thing has to be handed to a new
    person or rebuilt from nothing. <strong>Open the section you need.</strong></p>

    <details class="fold" id="broken-fold" open>
      <summary>When something breaks <span class="foldtag">read this first in a crisis</span></summary>
      <div class="foldbody" id="broken">
        <div class="tblwrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Most likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Site is down / error page</strong></td><td>Domain expired, or Cloudflare custom domain removed.</td><td>Check the domain at Network Solutions (renews Feb 2028). Then Cloudflare → website → Domains: both <code>brbcbaytown.org</code> and <code>www</code> should be listed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Site is fine but shows old content after a save</strong></td><td>Build failed, or your browser is caching.</td><td>Hard-refresh. Then Cloudflare → Deployments: if the top build is red, click <em>View build</em> and read the last lines of the log.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Site updates fine but <code>/admin</code> is dead, and the build log says "BUILDING WITHOUT THE EDITOR"</strong></td><td>Cloudflare's build variables were wiped or changed. The website keeps publishing on purpose; only editing stops.</td><td>Cloudflare → website → Settings → <strong>Build</strong> → <strong>Build variables</strong> must have <code>PUBLIC_TINA_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>TINA_TOKEN</code> (secret), <code>NODE_VERSION</code> = 22. This is a different box from the runtime "Variables and Secrets" panel — the build cannot see that one. Retry build.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Build is red for any other reason</strong></td><td>Something in the code.</td><td>Emergency valve: change the build command from <code>npm run build</code> to <code>npm run build:site</code>. That deploys the site without the editor, so the website is up while <code>/admin</code> is fixed. Then get help.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>/admin shows "Unexpected error validating your schema" or "Branch 'main' not found"</strong></td><td>TinaCloud lost its index of the code. Usually because <code>tina/tina-lock.json</code> was deleted, or someone clicked "Change Repo" in TinaCloud.</td><td>Make sure <code>tina/tina-lock.json</code> exists in the repo. In app.tina.io → Configuration → Refresh Branches; <code>main</code> should show a green check. <strong>Never click "Change Repo"</strong> — it replaces the whole project and invalidates every token.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>/admin login refused</strong></td><td>Wrong password on <code>connect@brbcbaytown.org</code>, or you are using a different address that was never invited, or the two seats are full.</td><td>Try <code>connect@brbcbaytown.org</code> with the normal ministry password first — that is the main login. Otherwise app.tina.io → Collaborators.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>New sermons stopped appearing</strong></td><td>The weekly job failed, or the video is unlisted/private (RSS only lists public videos).</td><td>GitHub → repo → Actions tab → "Sync sermons from YouTube". Red? Read the log. No key is involved — it reads the public RSS feed. You can run the job by hand from that tab.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Events page empty, or an event missing</strong></td><td>Not tagged <code>Website</code>, or the Planning Center token was revoked.</td><td>Check the tag first — it is almost always the tag. Otherwise Actions → "Sync events from Planning Center" and read the log. A new Personal Access Token from Planning Center goes into Secrets as <code>PCO_APP_ID</code> and <code>PCO_SECRET</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>A form button goes to a dead page</strong></td><td>Planning Center form deleted or renamed.</td><td>Get the new form address from Planning Center → Forms → View on Church Center. Paste it into Tina → Church Info → the matching <em>form link</em> field. All buttons update.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Giving panel blank</strong></td><td>Subsplash changed the embed address.</td><td>Subsplash → Giving → embed → new address into Tina → Church Info → Online giving link.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Rolling back a bad change: GitHub → the repo → Commits → find the last good one → the ⋯ menu on
        any commit lets you revert it. Cloudflare rebuilds automatically.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>What the website actually is, in one minute</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p><strong>brbcbaytown.org</strong> is a ten-page website. Its words live as plain text
        files in a code repository on GitHub. When somebody edits a word through the editor at
        <code>brbcbaytown.org/admin</code>, that saves a change to GitHub, GitHub tells Cloudflare,
        Cloudflare rebuilds the site — usually a few minutes, but allow ten — and the change is live. Nobody presses
        "publish". There is no database and no server to log into.</p>
        <p>Two things update themselves: <strong>sermons</strong> arrive from the church's YouTube
        channel every Monday, and <strong>events</strong> arrive from Planning Center every night —
        but only events someone has tagged <code>Website</code>. Giving runs through Subsplash. The
        two forms — "I'm interested" and "Join the serve team" — are Planning Center forms.</p>
        <p>It costs nothing to run. The only paid thing is the domain name, renewed every few years.</p>
        <h3>How a change gets onto the site</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>Someone edits a page at <code>brbcbaytown.org/admin</code> and clicks <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
          <li>Tina writes that change into the code repository on GitHub as a commit.</li>
          <li>GitHub tells Cloudflare a change landed.</li>
          <li>Cloudflare rebuilds the whole site from scratch. Usually a few minutes. It took ten on 10 September 2026, so do not panic at three.</li>
          <li>The new version goes live. Your browser may still show the old one for a minute — hard-refresh
            (<code>Cmd+Shift+R</code> on a Mac) or open a private window.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>You can watch step 4 happen at Cloudflare → Workers &amp; Pages → website → Deployments.
        A green check means it worked. A red X means the build failed and the site is still showing
        the previous version.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>There is no "publish" button, on purpose.</strong> Save is publish.
        If you save something by mistake, saving it back is the undo. Every version ever saved is kept in
        GitHub's history, so nothing is ever truly lost.</p></div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>Every system, and who owns it <span class="foldtag">ten pieces</span></summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p>Ten pieces. If you can find all ten, you have the whole site.</p>
        <div class="tblwrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>What it does</th><th>Where</th><th>Which account</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Domain name</strong></td><td>Owns <code>brbcbaytown.org</code>. Expires <strong>5 Feb 2028</strong>. Its nameservers point at Cloudflare.</td><td>Network Solutions</td><td>The church's registrar login</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>DNS + hosting</strong></td><td>Answers the domain and serves the site. Set up as a Worker named <code>website</code> with static assets. Rebuilds on every GitHub push.</td><td>dash.cloudflare.com</td><td>bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</td></tr>
