        </table></div>
        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>Where the actual passwords and keys are.</strong> Not here. They are on Matt Rose's
          Mac in a locked folder beside the project (<code>Baker Road Baptist/STORED ACCOUNTS/</code>),
          and the automation keys are in GitHub Secrets and Cloudflare's build variables, which cannot
          be read back once saved. The right long-term home is a shared password manager. If you are
          taking this over and cannot reach Matt, every one of these services has a normal
          "forgot password" flow on the account email above.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>The email accounts, and not getting locked out</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p>Four addresses, four jobs. Confusing them is the most common way to get locked out.
        The one an ordinary editor needs is <code>connect@brbcbaytown.org</code>; the rest are here
        so that whoever takes this over can find them.</p>
        <div class="tblwrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Job</th><th>Owns</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>bakerroadbaptist@gmail.com</code></td><td><strong>Build account.</strong> The infrastructure. Created for this project.</td><td>GitHub, Cloudflare, TinaCloud project, Google Cloud (YouTube API key)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>bakerroadbc@gmail.com</code></td><td><strong>The church's account.</strong> Public-facing.</td><td>Receives every "Email us" on the site.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>connect@brbcbaytown.org</code></td><td><strong>The editor login.</strong> Google Workspace on brbcbaytown.org. Uses the normal ministry password, so anyone in the office who already has that password can get in.</td><td>Signing in to <strong>brbcbaytown.org/admin</strong> via TinaCloud. This is the main login, the one to give a new person, and the one to change the password on when somebody leaves.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sarah@brbcbaytown.org</code></td><td><strong>Sarah Rose's account.</strong> Google Workspace on brbcbaytown.org.</td><td>Google Analytics (Viewer). Also holds the second TinaCloud editor seat, so she can edit alongside whoever is on <code>connect@</code>.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p><strong>Turn on two-factor authentication on the two Gmail accounts</strong>, and write the recovery codes
        down on paper somewhere in the church office. The realistic disaster is not a hacker; it is
        one person losing a phone and the church losing access to its own website.</p>
        <p>Add a second person as an admin on GitHub and Cloudflare. A church should never be one
        login away from losing its site.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>Where the code lives on this Mac — one copy, and only one</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p>Almost nobody needs this. Editors use <code>/admin</code> and never touch a file. But if you are
        editing the code directly, there is <strong>exactly one working copy on this machine</strong>:</p>
        <pre><code>~/Desktop/Second Brain/Baker Road Baptist/brbc-website</code></pre>
        <div class="callout warn">
          <p><strong>Do not make a second clone.</strong> There were two for a while — this one and another
          at <code>~/Desktop/brbc-website</code> — and by 10 September 2026 they had quietly drifted into a
          state where <em>neither one worked properly</em>. One held the Planning Center and TinaCMS
          credentials; the other held the YouTube API key and the OAuth token the sermon sync needs. Each
          copy could do half the job and neither could do all of it, and nothing warned anybody.</p>
          <p>The two were merged back together and the duplicate was removed. If you need the code on
          another machine, <code>git clone</code> it there and copy <code>.env</code> from STORED ACCOUNTS.
          Never keep a second copy on the same Mac.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>Rules that keep it from rotting</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>If a page will be wrong in six months unless somebody remembers to update it, it does not go on the website.</strong> It goes on Facebook or in the app. This rule is why the site went from 26 pages to 9 and why it will still be accurate in two years.</li>
          <li><strong>One name owns the site.</strong> Not a committee. Today that is Sarah Rose. Write the successor's name on paper when it changes.</li>
          <li><strong>Two Tina seats.</strong> Share one rather than pay for a third.</li>
          <li><strong>Never click "Change Repo" in TinaCloud.</strong> It does not refresh; it replaces.</li>
          <li><strong>Leave <code>--skip-cloud-checks</code> in the build command.</strong> It is what keeps the website deploying when the editor is misconfigured. A broken editor is a nuisance; a broken website is not.</li>
          <li><strong>The <code>STORED ACCOUNTS</code> folder never goes in the repository.</strong> It lives one folder above it for exactly that reason. Do not move it.</li>
          <li><strong>No passwords on this page.</strong> Ever.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="fold">
      <summary>The written documents</summary>
      <div class="foldbody">
        <p>All in the repository under <code>docs/</code>. Public, so these links work for anyone.</p>
        <div class="tblwrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>What it is for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
